/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (But what I've always liked about Dune is that it isn't your usual environmental cautionary tale: look at what we did to our planet. We've ruined it because of our greed. If only we cared more about our planet. I find that thinking overly simplistic, whether we're talking about Dune or our own planet.)
/E (But what I've always liked about Dune is that it isn't your usual environmental cautionary tale: look at what we did to our planet. We've ruined it because of our greed. If only we cared more about our planet. I find that thinking overly simplistic, whether we're talking about Dune or our own planet.)
/Pg 23 0 R
>>
endobj
318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Dune is not about the perils of turning out water world into a desert world. Actually, at the end of the Dune series, Arrakis ends up being a water world again, in part because of a desire to "save" the water. But it didn't work out well, because in the process, the spice on the planet was ruined forever, and along with it, its importance in the universe. It's not an either/or. Prioritizing water at whatever cost is just as overly idealistic and harmful as prioritizing spice at whatever cost.)
/E (Dune is not about the perils of turning out water world into a desert world. Actually, at the end of the Dune series, Arrakis ends up being a water world again, in part because of a desire to "save" the water. But it didn't work out well, because in the process, the spice on the planet was ruined forever, and along with it, its importance in the universe. It's not an either/or. Prioritizing water at whatever cost is just as overly idealistic and harmful as prioritizing spice at whatever cost.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
